                <head>205. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>Presidential Press Secretary James Hagerty’s diary entry for April 12–20, a
                    period which the President spent in Augusta, Georgia, states that Admiral Felix
                    B. Stump, Commander in Chief, Pacific, who was in Washington briefly for
                    consultations, met with the President on April 16. Hagerty’s diary entry reads
                    in part as follows:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“Stump flew down to Augusta, arriving at the airport at 8:00 A.M. Draper and
                        I met him and brought him to the National, arriving there at 8:30. The
                        President and Stump had a private conversation for one hour. It mainly dealt
                        with the build-up which the Chinese Communists are conducting on the
                        Mainland opposite Quemoy and Matsu. Aerial reconnaissance has shown that the
                        Communists are moving in fighter aircraft, including jets and moving some of
                        their bases. Neither Stump nor the President believe that an attack is
                        directly imminent, although the Chinese Corns are increasing their
                        potential.” (Eisenhower Library, Hagerty Papers)</p>
                </quote>
            </div>
